Subject: RE: Post-demo: technical sync — JSON schema safety & retry tasks

Samir / Asha — adding technical detail ahead of the deep-dive. Attaching a proposed schema (bf-proposed-schema.json) and a short note on retry logic (retry-patterns.md). High-level guidance below: 

1) Example schema (summary)\- order_summary: {
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["order_id","items","total","generated_at"],
  "properties": {
    "order_id": {"type":"string"},
    "items": {"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["sku","qty"]}},
    "total": {"type":"number"},
    "generated_at": {"type":"string","format":"date-time"},
    "source_trace": {"type":"object"} 
  }
}

2) Constrained decoding strategy\- two layers: (A) model-side prompt constraints: explicit response wrapper tokens + stop sequences; (B) Redwood validation: upon receipt we JSON-parse and run schema validation. If parse fails we will try 2 deterministic repairs (trim trailing tokens, attempt common escaping fixes) and re-validate. Repairs are logged with diffs.

3) Validation vs repair policy\- classify failures as: transient_parse_error (retryable), schema_violation_missing_required (non-retryable until prompt/params change), semantic_mismatch (requires human review). We can emit a machine-readable failure code for each. We do not auto-retry for missing required fields unless a safe fallback fill strategy is agreed and auditable.

4) Retry patterns (network/transient)\- recommended: up to 3 attempts with exponential backoff (100ms -> 250ms -> 600ms) + full jitter. For validation-retryable parse errors we attempt 1 immediate deterministic-repair attempt server-side before returning a failure to the client. See attached retry-patterns.md for examples and circuit-breaker thresholds.

5) Auth and routing\- SDK will support signed JWTs for the hosted path; for dedicated we recommend mutual TLS + optional customer-managed keys. Routing policy template: primary: hosted-eu (if tenant selects EU); fallback: dedicated-customer-cluster for backfills; emergency fallback: hosted-us with PII redaction if EU capacity degraded.

6) Embeddings and reranker\- if BrightFoundry runs embeddings in-house, Redwood will provide a lightweight embedding API spec and a 512-d float vector contract. For Redwood-managed embeddings: we recommend batched writes and asynchronous reranker jobs for throughput >300 req/s.

Attachments: bf-proposed-schema.json (sample schema), retry-patterns.md (pattern examples).

I can go deeper on SDK snippets — below is a short auth bootstrapping example for Node: 

// node snippet (pseudo)
const Redwood = require('redwood-sdk');
const client = new Redwood.Client({
  baseUrl: 'https://api.redwood.ai',
  auth: { type: 'jwt', tokenProvider: async() => await getSignedJwt() },
  region: 'eu',
});

// call with structured output enforcement
const res = await client.generate({
  model: 'redwood-structured-1',
  prompt: buildPrompt(input),
  structuredOutput: { schema: load('bf-proposed-schema.json'), enforce: true },
  routing: { primary: 'hosted-eu', fallback: 'dedicated-bf' },
});

Notes: we log validation errors with a unique failure id and payload sample (sanitized). That failure id can be surfaced to your team for audit.

Happy to walk through these items on the call — if you want me to drop deeper examples (e.g., the deterministic repair diffs) I can bring them.

